WEBVTT

00:00:00.281 --> 00:00:02.832
Last time, chromatography showed which dyes were in

00:00:02.872 --> 00:00:04.800
an ink, but not how much of each

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:05.362
one was there.

00:00:05.925 --> 00:00:08.174
Today we'll answer the how much question, with

00:00:08.215 --> 00:00:09.701
a mixture we can actually weigh.

00:00:10.406 --> 00:00:13.156
Here's twenty point zero zero grams of dry

00:00:13.197 --> 00:00:15.225
sand and table salt, stirred together.

00:00:15.948 --> 00:00:17.812
After we separate it, what fraction of this

00:00:17.874 --> 00:00:20.159
mixture was sand, and what fraction was salt?

00:00:20.822 --> 00:00:22.866
The answer comes from two kinds of measurement:

00:00:23.188 --> 00:00:25.072
the mass you start with, and the masses

00:00:25.113 --> 00:00:25.693
you get back.

00:00:26.555 --> 00:00:28.501
You've seen this separation before, so here it

00:00:28.562 --> 00:00:29.504
is in fast forward.

00:00:30.047 --> 00:00:32.240
Add water, and the salt dissolves while the

00:00:32.281 --> 00:00:32.864
sand doesn't.

00:00:33.548 --> 00:00:35.211
Pour it through filter paper, and the sand

00:00:35.252 --> 00:00:37.596
stays behind as the residue, while the salty

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:39.399
liquid, the filtrate, runs through.

00:00:40.122 --> 00:00:41.827
Rinse the sand with a little water to

00:00:41.888 --> 00:00:43.833
wash off any salt, then dry it.

00:00:44.435 --> 00:00:47.178
The filtrate goes into an evaporating basin, and

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.001
we heat it gently until most of the

00:00:49.043 --> 00:00:51.225
water has gone, then leave it somewhere warm

00:00:51.266 --> 00:00:51.946
to finish drying.

00:00:52.671 --> 00:00:55.007
Now each component sits in its own container.

00:00:55.657 --> 00:00:57.030
Here's a practical detail that matters.

00:00:57.415 --> 00:00:59.259
You can't tip the salt onto the balance,

00:00:59.641 --> 00:01:01.144
so you weigh its container too.

00:01:01.768 --> 00:01:04.522
The empty basin was forty-five point one two

00:01:04.563 --> 00:01:04.904
grams.

00:01:05.307 --> 00:01:07.056
With the dry salt in it, it reads

00:01:07.218 --> 00:01:08.826
fifty-nine point one two grams.

00:01:09.309 --> 00:01:12.096
Subtract, and the salt alone is fourteen point

00:01:12.197 --> 00:01:13.179
zero zero grams.

00:01:13.562 --> 00:01:15.195
That's called weighing by difference.

00:01:15.921 --> 00:01:17.723
The dried sand gets the same treatment on

00:01:17.764 --> 00:01:20.569
its watch glass: twenty-seven point three five grams,

00:01:20.911 --> 00:01:23.255
minus twenty-one point three five grams for the

00:01:23.336 --> 00:01:26.081
empty glass, gives six point zero zero grams

00:01:26.182 --> 00:01:26.562
of sand.

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:28.288
Now the fraction.

00:01:29.052 --> 00:01:32.119
Percentage by mass, which the syllabus calls percentage

00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:32.681
by weight,

00:01:33.222 --> 00:01:35.728
is the mass of one component, divided by

00:01:35.748 --> 00:01:38.054
the mass of the whole mixture, times one

00:01:38.134 --> 00:01:38.394
hundred.

00:01:39.176 --> 00:01:41.579
For the sand, that's six point zero zero

00:01:41.821 --> 00:01:44.464
over twenty point zero zero, times a hundred,

00:01:44.806 --> 00:01:46.728
which is thirty point zero percent.

00:01:47.532 --> 00:01:50.281
For the salt, fourteen point zero zero over

00:01:50.342 --> 00:01:53.392
twenty point zero zero gives seventy point zero

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:53.794
percent.

00:01:54.719 --> 00:01:57.210
And together they make one hundred percent, which

00:01:57.271 --> 00:01:58.174
is a useful check.

00:01:59.023 --> 00:02:01.258
Why divide by the twenty grams you started

00:02:01.298 --> 00:02:01.439
with?

00:02:02.123 --> 00:02:04.589
Because the question is about the original mixture,

00:02:05.012 --> 00:02:06.936
so the whole is what you weighed first.

00:02:07.843 --> 00:02:09.135
And here's why the check works.

00:02:10.002 --> 00:02:12.601
Filtering and evaporating are physical changes.

00:02:13.203 --> 00:02:14.805
They move the sand and the salt into

00:02:14.845 --> 00:02:17.867
different containers, but they don't create or destroy

00:02:17.968 --> 00:02:18.408
any matter.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.693
So if nothing is lost along the way,

00:02:21.035 --> 00:02:23.120
the masses you get back must add up

00:02:23.161 --> 00:02:24.383
to the mass you started with.

00:02:25.371 --> 00:02:27.446
Real practicals are rarely that tidy.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:31.056
Another group starts with twenty-five point zero zero

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.921
grams of the same kind of mixture.

00:02:33.623 --> 00:02:35.832
They get back eight point zero zero grams

00:02:35.873 --> 00:02:38.683
of sand, and sixteen point zero zero grams

00:02:38.745 --> 00:02:39.186
of salt.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.739
The sand is thirty-two point zero percent, and

00:02:42.761 --> 00:02:44.928
the salt is sixty-four point zero percent.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:48.124
Add those, and you've only accounted for ninety-six

00:02:48.186 --> 00:02:49.250
point zero percent.

00:02:49.955 --> 00:02:52.060
One gram, which is four point zero percent

00:02:52.081 --> 00:02:53.585
of the mixture, is missing.

00:02:54.251 --> 00:02:55.425
So where did it go?

00:02:56.149 --> 00:02:58.454
It didn't vanish, and it isn't a mystery

00:02:58.555 --> 00:02:59.336
third substance.

00:02:59.859 --> 00:03:01.609
It's mass that never made it into the

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:02.594
final containers.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.152
Some salty solution stays behind, wetting the beaker,

00:03:06.194 --> 00:03:07.379
the funnel and the filter paper.

00:03:08.262 --> 00:03:09.925
A little solution can spit out of the

00:03:09.966 --> 00:03:11.349
basin if it's heated too hard.

00:03:12.011 --> 00:03:14.016
And some crystals stay stuck to the glass

00:03:14.097 --> 00:03:14.979
rod or the basin.

00:03:15.882 --> 00:03:18.987
So report the missing percentage honestly, and name

00:03:19.048 --> 00:03:20.851
where the losses most likely happened.

00:03:22.018 --> 00:03:22.561
Quick prediction.

00:03:23.105 --> 00:03:25.271
Suppose a group weighs its sand while it's

00:03:25.312 --> 00:03:26.294
still a little damp.

00:03:27.038 --> 00:03:28.583
Will the percentage of sand come out too

00:03:28.644 --> 00:03:29.727
high, or too low?

00:03:30.593 --> 00:03:32.247
And what will the two percentages add up

00:03:32.288 --> 00:03:32.429
to?

00:03:33.296 --> 00:03:33.658
Have a think.

00:03:37.703 --> 00:03:38.126
Too high.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.081
The balance can't tell sand from water, so

00:03:41.101 --> 00:03:42.669
the water gets counted as sand.

00:03:43.191 --> 00:03:45.064
And that pushes the total above one hundred

00:03:45.084 --> 00:03:45.387
percent.

00:03:46.131 --> 00:03:47.698
So the total is a clue in both

00:03:47.758 --> 00:03:48.200
directions.

00:03:48.822 --> 00:03:50.670
Below one hundred means something was lost.

00:03:51.372 --> 00:03:53.899
Above one hundred means something extra was weighed,

00:03:54.279 --> 00:03:55.202
and it's usually water.

00:03:56.106 --> 00:03:58.040
The fix is drying to constant mass.

00:03:58.686 --> 00:04:00.612
Heat it, let it cool, and weigh it.

00:04:01.175 --> 00:04:03.561
Then heat and weigh again, until two readings

00:04:03.602 --> 00:04:04.584
in a row match.

00:04:05.438 --> 00:04:07.235
Here's the whole method in one line.

00:04:07.839 --> 00:04:10.548
Divide by what you started with, then account

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:11.110
for the rest.

00:04:11.854 --> 00:04:14.780
Each component's percentage uses the original mass on

00:04:14.801 --> 00:04:15.181
the bottom,

00:04:15.764 --> 00:04:17.587
and whatever is left over tells you about

00:04:17.669 --> 00:04:19.412
loss, or about extra mass.

00:04:22.086 --> 00:04:22.577
Your turn.

00:04:23.103 --> 00:04:25.634
A fifteen point zero zero gram mixture of

00:04:25.735 --> 00:04:27.663
chalk and table salt is separated.

00:04:28.266 --> 00:04:30.728
The student recovers four point two zero grams

00:04:30.768 --> 00:04:33.150
of dry chalk and ten point five zero

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:34.271
grams of dry salt.

00:04:34.992 --> 00:04:37.835
Calculate the percentage by mass of each component

00:04:37.915 --> 00:04:40.117
in the original mixture, and the percentage that

00:04:40.137 --> 00:04:40.978
was not recovered.

00:04:41.704 --> 00:04:42.615
Pause here to answer.

00:04:53.268 --> 00:04:56.116
Chalk is four point two zero over fifteen

00:04:56.178 --> 00:04:58.564
point zero zero, times a hundred, which is

00:04:58.686 --> 00:05:00.069
twenty-eight point zero percent.

00:05:00.772 --> 00:05:03.578
Salt is ten point five zero over fifteen

00:05:03.659 --> 00:05:06.445
point zero zero, which is seventy point zero

00:05:06.486 --> 00:05:06.846
percent.

00:05:07.508 --> 00:05:10.795
Together that's ninety-eight point zero percent, so two

00:05:10.876 --> 00:05:12.679
point zero percent was not recovered.

00:05:13.422 --> 00:05:15.887
The slip to avoid is dividing by fourteen

00:05:15.949 --> 00:05:18.013
point seven zero, the total you got back.

00:05:18.435 --> 00:05:20.064
That hides the loss completely.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:22.113
Last one.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.110
A ten point zero zero gram mixture of

00:05:25.211 --> 00:05:26.899
sand and salt is separated.

00:05:27.542 --> 00:05:29.245
The sand is dried in an oven for

00:05:29.347 --> 00:05:31.892
only ten minutes, then weighed at four point

00:05:32.013 --> 00:05:32.754
one two grams.

00:05:33.579 --> 00:05:35.872
The dry salt weighs six point two five

00:05:35.913 --> 00:05:36.194
grams.

00:05:37.019 --> 00:05:40.101
Calculate the percentage of each component, then explain

00:05:40.162 --> 00:05:42.583
why the total isn't one hundred percent, and

00:05:42.644 --> 00:05:43.703
how the method should change.

00:05:44.768 --> 00:05:45.636
Pause here to answer.

00:05:57.068 --> 00:05:59.492
Sand comes out at forty-one point two percent,

00:05:59.833 --> 00:06:02.137
and salt at sixty-two point five percent.

00:06:02.879 --> 00:06:04.807
That adds to one hundred and three point

00:06:04.848 --> 00:06:07.237
seven percent, so something extra was weighed.

00:06:07.981 --> 00:06:10.189
The most likely cause is water left in

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.117
the sand, because it was dried for only

00:06:12.178 --> 00:06:12.659
ten minutes.

00:06:13.544 --> 00:06:15.791
Dry it to constant mass before weighing.

00:06:16.615 --> 00:06:18.907
Calling the extra mass a third substance is

00:06:18.948 --> 00:06:19.832
the classic mistake.

00:06:21.761 --> 00:06:23.966
So by separating a mixture and weighing the

00:06:24.006 --> 00:06:25.951
parts, we found how much of it was

00:06:26.031 --> 00:06:26.312
salt.

00:06:26.935 --> 00:06:28.550
But salt itself is a compound.

00:06:29.054 --> 00:06:31.744
Its sodium and chlorine are bonded together in

00:06:31.784 --> 00:06:34.253
a fixed ratio, so no filter can pull

00:06:34.273 --> 00:06:34.734
them apart.

00:06:35.577 --> 00:06:37.822
Next time, we'll see how the formula alone

00:06:38.003 --> 00:06:40.187
tells us what fraction of a compound's mass

00:06:40.368 --> 00:06:41.450
comes from each element.

00:06:44.261 --> 00:06:45.084
Here are the key notes.

00:06:45.607 --> 00:06:47.155
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:47.195 --> 00:06:47.617
to copy them.
